needs:
title: What check does with a tracker a newer pidly wrote
effort: M
area: format
created: 2026-09-17T06:47:11Z
updated: 2026-09-18T21:57:25Z
handoff:

check is the only version-sensitive part of pidly. Everything else degrades
gracefully because it is files: an older build can still read, claim, close
and reopen a tracker written by a newer one.

check cannot, because it enforces a closed world. A field it has never heard
of is "not in schema.md", so any build older than 17 calls created and
updated undeclared, and after 28 an older build will not find .pidly/schema
at all and will treat every declared field as undeclared. The failure is
loud and wrong, and it happens to the person who cloned the repo rather than
to whoever upgraded.

The tracker must not record a format version. That is state that can
disagree with the files, which is the thing the format is built to avoid,
and it would have to be maintained by every hand-edit.

So the question is what check does about it:

- Nothing. Release notes name format changes and an old build is the user's
  problem. Cheapest, and the failure mode is a stranger's first run.
- Soften unknown fields from a finding to a note, keeping the nonzero exit
  for everything else. An unknown field is not damage — it is a field this
  build does not know about, which is exactly what a newer tracker looks
  like.
- Let check say it: when it finds fields it does not know, print one line
  suggesting the tracker may have been written by a newer pidly. Costs
  nothing and turns a confusing failure into an obvious one.

The second and third together look right, but this is a decision about what
check is for — it reports damage, and a version gap is not damage.

Bears on 29, which is what makes old builds reachable in the first place,
but does not depend on it.

Done when the decision is written down in the design and check behaves that
way, with a test that an unknown field does not fail a tracker whose other
findings are clean.


Decided 2026-09-18, and implemented. The second and third options together,
as the ticket expected.

check reports damage, and a version gap is not damage. So a field in no
schema is a note: printed, because an unknown field is worth seeing whether
it came from a newer build or from a typo, and not a reason to fail. Its
value is not read at all — nothing here knows what the field means, so
nothing here may judge it against another field's pattern.

When check sees one it says what it most likely is:

  note: 7: field 'severity' is not in the schema
  note: fields this build does not know
        — this tracker may have been written by a newer pidly,
          or the schema may be missing a stanza

A suggestion is all it can honestly be, and the reason is the one the ticket
gave: the tracker records no format version and must not, because a version
is state that can disagree with the files.

The case this exists for, measured on a tracker whose schema this build
cannot find — which is exactly what a build older than 28 sees:

  before   13 problems, twelve of them one per field per ticket, all wrong
  after     1 problem: the schema does not exist, which is true

Nothing else softened. A line that is not a field, a missing title:, a
required field with no value, a schema that is absent, an unparsable claim —
all still findings, all still nonzero.

The part the ticket did not name: edit. It reads everything info_faults
prints on stdout as a reason to refuse the save, so had the note gone there
an older build could never edit a newer tracker — the same stranger-hostile
failure, one command over. note() now writes to check's report when check is
the caller and to stderr when it is not, so edit saves a ticket carrying a
field it does not know, says so, and still refuses a file that is no longer
a ticket.

Written down in the design under "A field this build does not know", in the
schema section, and .pidly/README.md regenerated from it.

tests/newer.sh covers it: an unknown field does not fail an otherwise clean
tracker, is still printed as a note, and carries the suggestion; a real fault
alongside it still fails and the problem count counts only the fault; the
suggestion is absent when there is nothing to suggest; a tracker with no
schema at all reports one finding rather than one per field per ticket; edit
saves the unknown field and still refuses structural damage; and a value that
would fail another field's pattern is not judged by it.

portable.sh caught the first draft of that suite using sed's GNU-only "3a
text" — the guard 41 added, working on a file written after it.
